I. Adding the meta-productxy layer to your build
=================================================

Run 'bitbake-layers add-layer meta-productxy'

II. Misc
========

On Verdin iMX8MP with the Mallow device tree, the device-tree-overlays recipe
builds and enables verdin-imx8mp_spi1-gpio_overlay.dtbo. The u-boot-distro-boot
configuration also loads it after the existing HMP overlay.

Despite the retained spi1-gpio filename, this overlay now releases SPI_1
(ecspi1) for the M7, not Linux GPIO use. It disables the Linux SPI controller,
including spidev and the Mallow TPM, and does not apply pin mux settings.
The M7 firmware must configure ECSPI1 clocks, pad mux/control and chip selects:

  SODIMM 196: GPIO5_IO06 (SPI_1 clock)
  SODIMM 200: GPIO5_IO07 (SPI_1 MOSI)
  SODIMM 198: GPIO5_IO08 (SPI_1 MISO)
  SODIMM 202: GPIO5_IO09 (SPI_1 chip select 0)
  SODIMM  64: GPIO3_IO16 (Mallow TPM chip select 1)

Use the ECSPI1 alternate functions for clock, MOSI and MISO. SODIMM 202 can
use GPIO5_IO09 or ECSPI1_SS0, depending on the M7 driver. SODIMM 64 is the
TPM chip-select connection in the Mallow design; keep it inactive (high)
when addressing another SPI device if the TPM is populated.

Linux GPIO controllers remain enabled because other board functions use
them. This overlay does not enforce exclusive M7 access to individual GPIO
lines: do not use gpioset/gpioget or bind Linux drivers to these five pins.
Do not reset an entire shared GPIO bank from M7 firmware.

Build in the configured Yocto environment:

  bitbake device-tree-overlays u-boot-distro-boot
  bitbake torizon-docker

Deploy the rebuilt image and reboot. Verify with:

  gpioinfo | grep -E 'SODIMM_(196|200|198|202|64)"'

The lines should have no Linux consumer. /dev/spidev1.0 should no longer
exist. Verify SPI transfers using the M7 firmware and a logic analyzer;
the Linux overlay alone does not initialize or test M7 SPI operation.

Do not enable another overlay that re-enables ecspi1 after this overlay.
